{"name":"Solar Light","hex":"#faf0c9","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"solar","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of or pertaining to the sun; proceeding from the sun.","Born under the predominant influence of the sun."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Ellipsis of solar energy.","A fictional currency."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/solar"},{"term":"light","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Electromagnetic radiation in the wavelength range visible to the human eye (about 400–750 nanometers): visible light.","Electromagnetic radiation in the wavelength range visible to the human eye or in nearby ranges (infrared or ultraviolet radiation)."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To start (a fire).","To set fire to; to set burning."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/light"}],"entries":[{"title":"Electromagnetic spectrum","blocks":[{"text":"The electromagnetic spectrum is the full range of electromagnetic radiation, organized by frequency or wavelength. The spectrum is divided into separate bands, with different names for the electromagnetic waves within each band. From low to high frequency these are: radio waves, microwaves, infrared, visible light, ultraviolet, X-rays, and gamma rays. The electromagnetic waves in each of these bands have different characteristics, such as how they are produced, how they interact with matter, and their practical applications."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cf/EM_Spectrum_Properties_edit.svg/330px-EM_Spectrum_Properties_edit.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Electromagnetic_spectrum"},{"title":"Light","blocks":[{"text":"Light, visible light, or visible radiation is electromagnetic radiation that can be perceived by the human eye. Visible light spans the visible spectrum and is usually defined as having wavelengths in the range of 400–700 nanometers (nm), corresponding to frequencies of 750–420 terahertz. The visible band sits adjacent to the infrared and the ultraviolet, called collectively optical radiation."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fa/Prism_flat_rainbow_%28cropped%29.jpg/330px-Prism_flat_rainbow_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Light"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bf/Electromagnetic_spectrum%2C_NASA_illustration.jpg/500px-Electromagnetic_spectrum%2C_NASA_illustration.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bf/Electromagnetic_spectrum%2C_NASA_illustration.jpg/1280px-Electromagnetic_spectrum%2C_NASA_illustration.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bf/Electromagnetic_spectrum%2C_NASA_illustration.jpg/500px-Electromagnetic_spectrum%2C_NASA_illustration.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A visualization of the electromagnetic spectrum.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AElectromagnetic_spectrum%2C_NASA_illustration.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/ba/Rocca_dell%27Abisso%2C_Fondachelli_Fantina%2C_Sicilia.JPG/500px-Rocca_dell%27Abisso%2C_Fondachelli_Fantina%2C_Sicilia.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/ba/Rocca_dell%27Abisso%2C_Fondachelli_Fantina%2C_Sicilia.JPG/1280px-Rocca_dell%27Abisso%2C_Fondachelli_Fantina%2C_Sicilia.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/ba/Rocca_dell%27Abisso%2C_Fondachelli_Fantina%2C_Sicilia.JPG/500px-Rocca_dell%27Abisso%2C_Fondache